19.50.m:
A male horse is wealth, an acquisition;
a male gold ornament is golden;
place a male son, O herb,
into this woman.

With a horse, with an antelope, with a cow,
you have been bought, O herb;
a white horse is your wealth,
gold, greenish-yellow, is yours;
given here by King Soma,
become a producer of males.

19.51.m:
Whatever vow of mine has failed, O lord of vows,
may day and night put that together for me;
rising in the east, let the moon be the physician;
may the sun with his rays approve this.

May the beneficial wind blow upon the cows;
may they graze on the nourishing herbs;
fat, granting living wealth, bringing joy—
have mercy, O Rudra, upon the four-footed pasturage.

Extend the trace for fortune;
raise the goad for fortune;
for fortune, strike the right [beast]
of the yoked draft-team with the goad!

19.52.m:
One fit to deceive has not deceived me, [who am] hard to deceive;
I am neither naive by birth nor a novice.
I am the overpowerer, the all-conquering, conquering armies, the sun-finder;
what this whole universe is, that am I.

The inspired poet has stepped forth, has stood up, was born;
he measured out the width of the earth.
The universal ruler took his seat over all beings;
he indeed knows the vows of Varuṇa.

You are born of Indra, born of Soma;
him we send away forth from here.
The fish [is in] the wide sweet water;
you drink up its overflowing,
free from discharge, free from sickness.

19.54.m:
Awaken him, awaken [him] fully;
do not permit [him] to sleep.
Standing upright, he shall think of me;
do not mention even [the name] of other women!

19.55.m:
With a backward-directed blow I stop up his
out-breath and his in-breath, whoever would seek to seize me;
let it cut off his life as an axe cuts a tree,
so that he may not live to the third dawn.

Furthering with the oblation, O Prajāpati, I invest the couple with prosperity.
Grant increase of wealth, O Savitṛ;
make [them] dying only of old age, with all their heroes.

19.56.m:
With an oblation endowed with offspring I sacrifice for you:
place an embryo in the womb;
let it be born at the proper time again,
in the tenth month to bring forth.

With an oblation having good sons I sacrifice for you:
place an embryo in the womb;
let it be born at the proper time again,
in the tenth month to bring forth.

20.1.m:
The son knows the father, he [knows] the mother;
he became the son, he became one of renewed bounty.
He covered heaven, the intermediate space, he the light of heaven;
he became all existences; he came into being.

Some of yours are auspicious, some of yours are inauspicious;
you bear all, not taking offense.
These three [parts] of Speech are placed in secret;
one only has spread through all this entirely.

Aditi is heaven, Aditi is intermediate space,
Aditi is mother, he is father, he is son;
Aditi is all the gods, the five peoples;
Aditi is what has been born, Aditi is what is to be born.

Of the sons of Diti, of Aditi I have sung,
possessing great shelter, great, without blemish;
for their oceanic domain is deep;
nothing is beyond their trembling power.

20.2.m:
The sacrifice came into being;
it came forth into being;
it became the overlord of the earth.
It was born forth, and it grew again;
may it place wealth among us.

20.3.m:
This is your oblation, rich in ghee, O Sarasvatī;
this is what belongs to the ancestors.
These songs of yours are most beneficial;
through them may we be generous patrons.

May Pūṣan follow our cows;
may Pūṣan protect our coursers;
may Pūṣan win the prize for us.

O Pūṣan, under your ordinance
may we never suffer harm;
we are your praisers here.

Bright is one [form] of yours, worthy of worship is your other;
of different forms are your two days, like heaven you are.
For you protect all miraculous powers, O self-willed one;
blessed, O Pūṣan, be your gift here.

The Creator rules over offspring and wealth;
the Creator brought forth this whole world.
May he bring abundant growth to the donor;
to that god we would worship with oblation.

20.4.m:
Heat did not scorch, cold did not strike;
the earth, quick with rain, stretches forth.
Even the waters flow ever for him;
where soma is, there is ever good fortune.

20.5.m:
May the goddess Anumati come to us,
she of good rule, of hero-strength, well-born;
for her foremost thought has become auspicious;
may she help this sacrifice enjoyed by the gods.

This is the thought of sages,
light in the distribution;
may the ruddy [sun] stir the concordant dawns.

20.6.m:
Agree together, unite together;
let your minds understand together.
May Mitra unite all of you;
let there be harmony with me for you.

20.7.m:
O gods, grant shelter to us for both kinds of wealth,
for two-footed and four-footed,
eating, drinking, strengthening, well-fed;
bestow that disease-free well-being and fortune upon us.

O Bṛhaspati, O Savitṛ, awaken him;
sharpen further what is already sharpened;
increase him for great good fortune;
all the gods rejoice after him.

20.8.m:
Right here seize him, O Nirṛti;
right here destroy him, O Nirṛti; right here cut him down.
Be full of sweetness for me on this side here;
be right in the middle for that one on that side.

20.9.m:
O Agni, be bold for great good fortune;
may your splendors be supreme.
Bring the marital state well under control;
tread down the powers of those who act as enemies.

O Agni, drive away the rivals that are born;
drive back those that are unborn, O Jātavedas.
Speak favorably for us, being well-disposed;
grant us triple-guarded, breaking-through shelter.

Overcome the other rivals with force;
crush those unborn, O Jātavedas.
Fill this realm for good fortune;
let the Vasu gods rejoice along with you.

20.10.m:
Cast that one down below us, O Agni;
weaken the puffed-up rival.
Whoever would injure us, may they be down below;
wipe away all hatreds all around.

20.11.m:
Walk together, do not abandon the body;
prāṇa and apāna, be united here.
Go forth from the Seven Ṛṣis;
let them lead this man in well-being to old age.

May the prāṇa not abandon you that has entered into you;
may the apāna not go afar, leaving you behind.
Recovering yourself, live for autumns, full of splendor;
Agni is your protector, the most excellent overlord.

Rākā, easy to invoke, I invoke with good praise;
may she, fortunate, hear us and take note of her own accord.
May she sew the work with an unbroken needle;
may she give a hero of a hundred gifts, worthy of praise.

20.12.m:
Agni is kindled, O Aśvins;
heated is the gharma for you, come here.
Now the cows are being milked here, O bulls;
the skilled singers speak, O wonder-workers.

20.13.m:
Be auspicious to us, most comforting,
very gracious, O Sarasvatī;
may we not be separated from the sight of you.

20.15.m:
With Indra as ally, as companion,
I drive away the attackers;
let the enemies be underneath.

20.17.m:
In me be luster, in me fame,
in me brilliance, in me vigor;
I take away your luster;
may I be supreme.

20.18.m:
May Savitṛ impel distress away from the feet,
Varuṇa, Mitra, Aryaman away from the hands;
away the Ādityas, Anumati granting gifts;
may Savitṛ impel us forward to good fortune.

A hundred and one signs
are born here together at a mortal’s birth;
the most evil of them we send away from here;
give us auspicious ones, O Jātavedas.

20.20.m:
Not slaying the husband's brother, O Varuṇa;
not slaying the husband, O Bṛhaspati;
not slaying the sons, O Indra;
impel those signs of fortune to her, O Savitṛ.

May the earth strike away what is inauspicious from the feet;
Varuṇa, Mitra, Aryaman [strike it] away from the hands;
away the Ādityas, Anumati granting gifts;
may Savitṛ impel us forward to good fortune.

She who has become wandering between both,
the one enlivens the other, auspicious and evil;
that sign [of misfortune] we take away with an invocation;
[bring] the auspicious, very auspicious one for good fortune.

20.21.m:
The signs of fortune have come together;
together have the signs of fortune come.
The auspicious signs of fortune have conquered;
the evil ones have been conquered and driven away.

20.22.m:
As I speak, may all speak after me;
as I breathe, may all breathe after me;
as I enter, may all enter after me;
may the gods be united in me alone.

May Sūrya protect me from [loss of] sight,
Bṛhaspati from [loss of] speech, King Soma from the assembly;
may I become Indra for you to behold.

To this better dwelling have I come;
auspicious have heaven and earth become for me.
May the quarters be free of rivals for me;
truly we do not hate you; may we have safety.

20.23.m:
O Lord of the Homestead, grant us shelter here;
lead us from good to better, to wealth.
May my heroes be uninjured here;
may two-footed and four-footed nourishment be in me.

Mount the pillars by cosmic order, O roof-beam;
mighty, ruling widely, keep off the enemies.
May your attendants not be harmed here;
may one live hundreds of autumns among rulers.

Through you they conquer the truth;
yours is the true arbitration.
You are born at the top of the tree;
as such, give birth to this man.

Do not leave consumption here;
do not go apart murmuring.
Grant us good fortune;
let guilt follow you.

20.26.m:
Unite me with fortune, with twofold splendor;
unite me with the earth, unite me with the herbs;
may the refreshing waters
anoint me with fortune and with splendor.

20.27.m:
Transverse let your embryo be;
let your afterbirth remain in the heart.
Then quickly mount the fire;
then go to the seat of Yama.

Brace apart with the brace;
make the two go in different directions.
This fire is between you two;
let it cast you two in different directions.

I send you to the ocean;
go into your own origin.
May I be uninjured in body;
let not my milk be poured away.

The sage inscribed in the beginning;
the Sustainer held the hairs;
setting [them] from all around right here,
Dhātṛ, Tvaṣṭṛ arranged the hairs in the skin.

20.28.m:
Let the arrows of the gods slay him;
let the priests slay him with wrath,
who, O Prajāpati, stands between
us and our fire.

20.29.m:
Stand up, run out;
there is no hiding place for you here.
Toward the property of that man I urge you.

20.31.m:
I praise Agni of good wealth with homages;
here engaged, may he perceive what we have done.
As with chariot-steeds seeking booty, I bring forward;
may I perform auspiciously the praise of the Maruts.

May the eyes of both of us look like honey;
may our face be ointment.
Put me within your heart;
may our mind be together.

20.33.m:
Ghee is in your divine seat, O Agni;
with ghee Manu kindles you today.
Ghee the watery gods bring to you;
ghee the cows yield for you at the beginning.

Go away! An enemy you are, or an enemy you are!
You have mixed poison into the poison;
go away against the serpent himself, slay him!

20.34.m:
May the wind blow blessedly on me;
may the sun shine blessedly for me.
May the days be blessed for me;
may the night be set down blessedly;
may the dawn shine brightly with blessing for me.

Stand up, O Fathers who were here before;
praise king Yama, the resting place.
This most manly of men, the best, has arrived;
make space for him according to his kinship.

The cows have gone to their abode;
the bird has gone to its dwelling.
The mountains have stood firm in their foundation;
I have brought the two kidneys to rest in their place.

This whole world has stood still;
the forest-shaking wind has stood still;
the trees whose sleep is upright have stood still;
may this disease of yours stand still.

20.35.m:
May this sacrifice of ours reach the gods;
may the vedi reach the higher vedi.
I dismiss the priests with their fees;
O gods, sacrifice again in the higher stage.

O sacrifice, go to the sacrifice;
go to the sacrificer;
go to your own womb! Svāhā!

20.37.m:
You have three horns, difficult to assail;
you are held for Indra;
Pūṣan is your eater.

Desire has entered into you, O queen;
I call it out again.
May my mind be upon house and cattle;
may good fortune be in my eyes;
may savor be on my tongue;
may strength be in my arms;
may speed be in my thighs.

You are churned out of heaven,
brought up from the earth;
you are spoken of as born from the sea;
you are an Atharvanic remedy for eye-disease.

May Pūṣan turn hither from beyond
the four directions of the earth;
may what is lost come back to us;
let us enjoy it alive.

20.58.m:
For long life, for ascetic heat,
for my safety from harm,
the eagle spoke to me:
"This is the remedy for falling off;
this is the remedy for puliśa."

20.59.m:
The heart of yours that is well-parted,
that indeed is in Prajāpati;
we know that [heart] of yours;
(may I not fall into misfortune concerning sons).

You are Agni's heart;
the lightning knows you thus;
may I not fall into misfortune concerning sons.

20.60.m:
You are resting places, you are delights;
you have rested on me, rest on me!
You are named the longing thoughts, the village Apsarases;
I know your possession; enter into me!

20.61.m:
This Agni is our overseer;
this one is our best obtainer of wealth;
may we not suffer harm in approaching him;
may he protect our offspring!

20.62.m:
May the Maruts sprinkle me,
may the two months, the reddish [dawns] of heaven;
may this Agni sprinkle me
with offspring and with wealth;
may he make long life for me.

20.63.m:
May the facial blemish fly away from me,
like a cloud carried off by the wind;
may golden beauty come to me;
may my face be fortunate.
